    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s3-n19",
        "locale": "zh",
        "text": "# 19. 机械振动和电振荡之间的相似性\n\n> 振动和波 | 第一章　机械振动\n\n振荡回路中的电振荡与弹簧振子的自由振动相似。\n两者发生周期性变化的物理量不同：机械振动中变化的是物体的坐标 $x$ 和速度投影 $v_x$；\n电振荡中变化的是电容器的电量 $q$ 和电路中的电流强度 $i$。\n\n这些量的物理意义不同，但它们随时间发生周期性变化的过程相似。\n这是因为两种振动的产生条件有相似之处。\n使弹簧振子回到平衡位置的是弹性力 $F_x$。\n\n弹性力的大小与振子偏离平衡位置的位移大小成正比，比例系数是弹簧的刚度 $k$。\n电容器极板间的电压 $U$ 决定了电容器的放电，也就是电流的出现。\n这个电压与电量 $q$ 成正比，因为 $U=\\frac{1}{C}q$。\n\n这里的比例系数是电容的倒数 $\\frac{1}{C}$。\n\n由于惯性，振子的速度在力的作用下只能逐渐增大；\n作用力消失时，速度也不会立即变为零。\n与此相似，由于自感，线圈中的电流在电压的作用下逐渐增大；\n\n电压变为零时，电流也不会立即消失。\n回路的电感系数 $L$ 对应振子的质量 $m$。\n比较振子的动能 $\\frac{mv_x^2}{2}$ 和电流的能量 $\\frac{Li^2}{2}$，就能看出这两个量在能量表达式中的对应关系。\n\n给电容器充电，相当于用手使振子偏离平衡位置。\n振子的初始位移为 $x_{\\mathrm{m}}$，弹簧振子因此具有势能 $\\frac{kx_{\\mathrm{m}}^2}{2}$（图 $28$b）。\n把这个表达式与电容器的能量 $\\frac{q_{\\mathrm{m}}^2}{2C}$ 比较，可以看出弹簧刚度 $k$ 对应电容的倒数 $\\frac{1}{C}$。\n\n振子的初始坐标 $x_{\\mathrm{m}}$ 对应电容器的初始电量 $q_{\\mathrm{m}}$。\n\n由电势差引起的电流，对应振子在弹性力作用下获得的速度 $v_x$（图 $29$b）。\n交变电流在某一时刻的电流强度，可以用通过电量对时间的导数表示：\n$i=\\lim_{\\Delta t \\to 0}\\frac{\\Delta q}{\\Delta t}=q'$。\n\n下表汇总了机械振动与电振荡中的对应关系。\n\n表\n"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s3-n19",
        "locale": "en",
        "text": "# 19. Similarities Between Mechanical and Electrical Oscillations\n\n> Oscillations and Waves | Chapter 1: Mechanical Oscillations\n\nElectrical oscillations in an oscillating circuit are similar to the free oscillations of a spring oscillator, an object attached to a spring.\nDifferent physical quantities change periodically, which means their changes repeat: in mechanical oscillations, the changing quantities are the object's position coordinate $x$ and its velocity component $v_x$, which gives its speed and direction along the chosen axis;\n\nin electrical oscillations, they are the charge $q$ stored in the capacitor and the current $i$ flowing in the circuit.\nThese quantities have different physical meanings, but the ways they change periodically over time are similar.\n\nThis is because the conditions that produce the two kinds of oscillation are similar.\nThe elastic force $F_x$, exerted by the spring, brings the spring oscillator back toward its equilibrium position, where the spring exerts no net restoring force.\n\nThe farther the oscillator moves from equilibrium, the greater the elastic force: the force's magnitude is proportional to the size of the displacement, with spring stiffness $k$ as the constant of proportionality.\nThe voltage $U$ between the capacitor's plates causes the capacitor to discharge, so current begins to flow.\n\nThis voltage is proportional to the charge $q$, because $U=\\frac{1}{C}q$.\nHere the constant of proportionality is the reciprocal of the capacitance, $\\frac{1}{C}$: the capacitance tells us how much charge the capacitor stores for a given voltage.\n\nBecause of inertia, the object's tendency to keep its motion, a force can increase the oscillator's speed only gradually;\nwhen the force stops acting, the velocity does not immediately become zero.\n\nSimilarly, because of self-induction, in which a changing current produces an effect that opposes that change, the current in a coil increases gradually under an applied voltage;\nwhen the voltage becomes zero, the current does not immediately disappear.\n\nThe circuit's inductance $L$, which describes the coil's opposition to changes in current, corresponds to the oscillator's mass $m$.\nComparing the oscillator's kinetic energy $\\frac{mv_x^2}{2}$ with the energy of the current's magnetic field, $\\frac{Li^2}{2}$, shows how these quantities correspond in the energy formulas.\n\nCharging the capacitor corresponds to moving the oscillator away from equilibrium by hand.\nIts initial displacement is $x_{\\mathrm{m}}$, so the spring oscillator has potential energy $\\frac{kx_{\\mathrm{m}}^2}{2}$, energy stored by stretching or compressing the spring (Figure $28$b).\n\nComparing this expression with the capacitor's energy $\\frac{q_{\\mathrm{m}}^2}{2C}$ shows that spring stiffness $k$ corresponds to the reciprocal of capacitance, $\\frac{1}{C}$.\nThe oscillator's initial coordinate $x_{\\mathrm{m}}$ corresponds to the capacitor's initial charge $q_{\\mathrm{m}}$.\n\nThe current caused by a potential difference, or voltage, corresponds to the velocity $v_x$ that the oscillator gains under the spring's elastic force (Figure $29$b).\nAt a particular instant, an alternating current can be expressed as the time derivative of transferred charge: the rate at which charge passes, found by considering shorter and shorter time intervals, with transferred charge counted positively in the chosen positive current direction.\n$i=\\lim_{\\Delta t \\to 0}\\frac{\\Delta q}{\\Delta t}=q'$.\n\nThe table below summarizes the corresponding quantities in mechanical and electrical oscillations.\n\nTable\n"
      }
    ],
